<!-- ============ FAQ ============ -->
<section class="section" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head center reveal">
      <div class="kicker-row center"><span class="eyebrow muted"><i data-lucide="help-circle"></i> Common questions</span></div>
      <h2 class="h2">Good to know.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq reveal">
      <details open><summary>Is it really free? <i data-lucide="plus" class="plus"></i></summary><div class="ans">Yes — Safety Intercept is completely free to install and use. No trial, no credit card, no hidden upgrade to "unlock" protection.</div></details>
      <details><summary>Can you see my bank account or move my money? <i data-lucide="plus" class="plus"></i></summary><div class="ans">No. Safety Intercept reads the payment or email <em>on the page you're already looking at</em> to spot scam patterns. It has no access to your bank, your balance, or your ability to move money.</div></details>
      <details><summary>What does it actually check? <i data-lucide="plus" class="plus"></i></summary><div class="ans">It looks at the things scammers rely on: brand-new recipients, urgent "pay now" pressure, spoofed sender addresses, suspicious links, and gift-card or wire requests — then explains what it found in plain English.</div></details>
      <details><summary>Will it slow me down or get in the way? <i data-lucide="plus" class="plus"></i></summary><div class="ans">No. It stays invisible until it sees something risky. When it does step in, you can dismiss the warning in one tap and carry on.</div></details>
      <details><summary>Which sites does it support? <i data-lucide="plus" class="plus"></i></summary><div class="ans">Today: PayPal, Wells Fargo / Zelle, and Gmail. More banks and email providers are on the way.</div></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

